{"id":"status-granted-by-notice","title":"Permanent status granted by notice, with no examination","tier":"OFFICIAL","question":"How often did a classification notice give employees permanent civil-service status in a class they had not been examined for, and how far back was it dated?","finding":"11 notices of fiscal 2009 to 2012 grant permanent status 22 times: 20 grants name employees (48 names in all, a few of them twice), and 2 cover every permanent employee of a hospital laundry class under an arbitration award. 5 moved a single employee into a management class; 5 grants were dated back, the longest by 10.4 years.","how":"Every “Grant status” section of the notices saved for fiscal 2008-09 to 2015-16. People are counted from the lettered list of names or, where one person is named, as one; names are not kept. Years back are from the date the notice took effect to the date the grant was made retroactive to.","limits":"Granting status when a position is reallocated to a new class is how the Civil Service Rules move an incumbent whose duties already match it; it is not a short cut by itself. A retroactive date usually marks when the duties changed, and the notice does not say whether back pay followed. Notices after fiscal 2015-16 were not read.","columns":[{"k":"date","t":"Notice took effect","type":"date"},{"k":"from","t":"From class","type":"text"},{"k":"to","t":"Status granted in","type":"text"},{"k":"who","t":"Who","type":"text"},{"k":"retro","t":"Dated back to","type":"date"},{"k":"years","t":"Years back","type":"int"},{"k":"basis","t":"Stated basis","type":"text"},{"k":"notice","t":"Notice","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"date":"2008-09-05","from":"Forester (class 3426)","to":"Manager I (class 0922)","who":"1 named permanent employee","retro":"1998-03-27","years":10.4,"basis":"Not stated","notice":{"t":"Notice","u":"https://sfdhr.org/Admin/Modules/ShowDocument.aspx?documentid=2324"}},{"date":"2010-02-09","from":"IS Manager (class 1071)","to":"Manager VI (class 0